.TH WTWMRC 5 "2026-08-18" "wtwm 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
wtwmrc \- configuration file for wtwm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wtwm
reads the twm configuration language.  The goal is to let an existing
.I .twmrc
control native Wayland and Xwayland windows without renaming or destructively
migrating that file.  Keywords and action names are case-insensitive; quoted
names and command arguments retain their text.
.PP
The reference grammar is the grammar of twm 1.0.13.1.  This page documents how
to use that grammar under Wayland and calls out the operational differences a
user must understand.  The installed compatibility ledger gives the status of
every recognized directive and action.
.SH SEARCH ORDER
For screen zero and no explicit command-line file, wtwm tries these sources in
order and uses the first readable one:
.IP 1. 4
.I $HOME/.twmrc.0
.IP 2. 4
.I $HOME/.twmrc
.IP 3. 4
the packaged
.I system.twmrc
.IP 4. 4
compiled-in defaults
.PP
An explicit
.B wtwm -f file
tries
.I file
first, then the packaged file, then compiled-in defaults.  Set
.B WTWM_SYSTEM_CONFIG
only to override the packaged path for a controlled build or test.
.SH LEXICAL FORM
Blank lines and whitespace separate tokens.
.B #
introduces a comment outside a quoted string.  Strings use double quotes and
support the reference escapes and continued lines.  Integer, signed-integer,
geometry, color, cursor, context, modifier, and action productions follow twm.
Malformed input is rejected atomically; a failed runtime reload leaves the
active configuration intact.
.SH TOP-LEVEL FORMS
The principal forms are:
.TP
.I Keyword
A boolean setting, for example
.BR OpaqueMove ,
.BR NoTitleFocus ,
or
.BR DecorateTransients .
.TP
.I Keyword value
A scalar setting such as
.BR BorderWidth ,
.BR MoveDelta ,
.BR ConstrainedMoveTime ,
font names, cursor names, or geometry.
.TP
.I "Keyword { entries }"
A color, monochrome, pixmap, squeeze-title, icon-manager, or window match list.
.TP
.B Function
.I "name { actions }"
Defines an ordered reusable action sequence; the name is quoted in the file.
.TP
.B Menu
.I "name { label action ... }"
Defines a quoted-name menu.  An entry may invoke an action, function, or
submenu.
.TP
.I "ButtonN = modifiers : contexts : action"
Binds pointer button
.I N
to an action.
.TP
.I "key = modifiers : contexts : action"
Binds a quoted key name to an action.
.PP
Use
.B wtwm-config file
to validate all forms and inspect the resulting configuration before login.
.SH MODIFIERS AND CONTEXTS
The reference modifier names
.BR s ,
.BR c ,
.BR m ,
.BR l ,
numeric modifier selectors, and combinations separated by
.B |
are accepted.  Contexts include
.BR root ,
.BR window ,
.BR title ,
.BR icon ,
.BR iconmgr ,
.BR frame ,
and
.BR all .
Context lists use
.BR | .
.PP
On Wayland the compositor consumes bindings before a client receives them, just
as twm consumes its window-manager bindings.  A binding without an appropriate
context does not become a global client shortcut.
.SH WINDOW MATCHING
Window lists retain twm's ordered name/class behavior.
.IP \(bu 2
For Xwayland, wtwm checks
.BR WM_NAME ,
then the instance and class strings in
.BR WM_CLASS .
.IP \(bu 2
For native Wayland, wtwm checks the xdg-shell title, then
.BR app_id .
.PP
Applications choose their own Wayland
.BR app_id .
Inspect application documentation or diagnostic output rather than assuming it
equals the executable name.  Title and identity changes are reapplied where the
corresponding twm rule is dynamic.
.SH COLORS, FONTS, AND PIXMAPS
.B Color
and
.B Monochrome
blocks use twm color names and per-window override lists.  Color, grayscale, and
monochrome compositor modes can be selected with
.BR wtwm --visual-mode .
X core bitmap font descriptions are resolved to matching installed fonts for
classic metrics.  If an exact legacy font is missing, install the expected font
package or choose an available Fontconfig/Pango description.
.PP
XBM files used for title buttons, icons, and pixmaps are decoded with bounded
dimensions and input sizes.  Relative paths are resolved according to the
configuration's pixmap path rules.  A malformed bitmap is rejected or reported;
it is never treated as executable content.
.SH PLACEMENT AND OUTPUTS
twm's screen is translated to the selected Wayland output.  Initial placement,
move constraints, zoom variants, icon regions, and icon managers operate in
that output's logical coordinates.  An X11 position request keeps its global
coordinates where the reference accepts it.  Native Wayland has no client
position hint: unparented windows use the pointer output, and transient windows
use their managed parent output.
.PP
The reference multi-screen actions are translated to the configured Wayland
output order.  Output removal repairs visibility of managed windows; output
return restores eligible prior placement.  See the compatibility ledger for
the exact translation of each screen action.
.SH ACTIONS AND COMMANDS
The complete reference action namespace is recognized.  Effective actions
cover focus, stacking, move/resize, zoom/fill, iconify, menus, icon managers,
output traversal, configuration reload, session state, clipboard/cut-buffer
translation, client close, command execution, and compositor exit.
.PP
.B f.exec
runs the configured text with the user's privileges.  Simple quoted or escaped
argument vectors execute directly.  Text requiring shell syntax is passed
unchanged to
.BR /bin/sh -c .
Review commands in any imported configuration.
.PP
.B f.delete
requests a graceful client close.
.B f.destroy
cannot forcibly kill a native xdg-shell client and is translated to the
available close request.  Xwayland behavior follows the documented ICCCM path.
.PP
.B f.restart
and
.B f.twmrc
reload configuration atomically without replacing the Wayland compositor
process or disconnecting clients.  This is the Wayland-safe observable
equivalent of twm restart behavior.
.PP
.B f.saveyourself
writes wtwm's explicit persistent snapshot and also uses the X11
.B WM_SAVE_YOURSELF
protocol when an Xwayland target supports it.  Ordinary logout and crashes do
not replace the snapshot.
.SH X11-ONLY DIRECTIVES
Server grabs, backing-store preferences, save-unders, installed colormaps, ring
bells, and several X resource choices have no native Wayland protocol.  wtwm
either reproduces their visible result in compositor-owned state, preserves the
Xwayland behavior where possible, or accepts them as verified no-ops.  It does
not silently assign an unrelated meaning.  Consult the compatibility ledger
before relying on one of these directives.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
# Keep ordinary twm syntax; native applications match title then app_id.
OpaqueMove
DecorateTransients
MoveDelta 3

Function "move-or-raise" { f.move f.deltastop f.raise }
Button1 = m : window|icon : f.function "move-or-raise"
"Return" = m : all : f.exec "foot"

Menu "operations"
{
    "Operations" f.title
    "Move"       f.move
    "Resize"     f.resize
    "Iconify"    f.iconify
    "Terminal"   f.exec "foot"
    "Exit"       f.quit
}
Button1 = : root : f.menu "operations"
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.twmrc.0
Screen-zero configuration.
.TP
.I $HOME/.twmrc
General user configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wtwm/system.twmrc
Packaged sample and fallback.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/wtwm/state
Explicit state snapshot used with
.BR RestartPreviousState .
.SH MIGRATION
Keep the original file, validate it with
.BR wtwm-config (1),
then test wtwm nested before choosing a login session.  Retain the old X11 twm
session until native and Xwayland application identities, fonts, commands, and
output behavior have been checked.  The installed migration and troubleshooting
guides provide a reversible procedure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wtwm (1),
.BR wtwm-config (1),
.BR twm (1)
